VIOLATION — the artifact fails the meta-rules that govern rule authoring, and fails them on
content, not on shape. Its substantive defect is that it inverts the very contract it claims to
express: conservation-gate.md's second MUST NOT forbids closing a boundary operation whose
enumeration never ran "on the grounds that the operation is routine", and this artifact writes
that ground into the rule as an exemption ("Routine operations are normally exempt"). It goes
further and licenses the two instruments the parent contract forbids — treating a success report
plus a consistent-looking result as sufficient ("the agent may treat the transfer as accounted for
on that basis"), and endorsing NAME comparison ("a path that is still present ... will normally
indicate that the underlying content came through"), which is MUST-2's DO-NOT stated as a DO.

AUTHORING DEFECTS COMPOUND IT. The section is headed "MUST Rules" while the primary modal
throughout is should/normally/generally/may — no clause carries binding force. The DO/DO-NOT block
restates the heading in prose instead of showing two commands whose outcomes differ, so it teaches
nothing. The BLOCKED list is abstract paraphrase of failure modes rather than matchable agent
speech, so no agent utterance can ever be recognized against it. The Why asserts budget cost and
closes on "routine transfers are mostly fine", which is the belief under test rather than a
consequence of breach.

NOT DEFECTS. The Wiring block is present and carries the canonical field set; its `advisory`
severity follows from the inverted premise and is not a separate finding. The frontmatter, the
Origin section and the heading skeleton are well-formed and match its counterpart exactly — the
pair is deliberately equalized on surface so that neither size, section list nor furniture can
answer the question.
